The poem then runs a striking contrast. Halitherses warns the assembly again in Book 24, this time against launching another revenge expedition. More than half leave rather than follow Eupeithes \citep{HomerMurray1919}. The same kind of speech by the same interpreter now changes behavior. What has changed is not merely the content of the warning. The evidence environment has changed. Halitherses has been vindicated. Bodies are present. Consequence has raised the credibility of the interpreter of consequence.

This means warning has at least four possible functions in the poem. It can guide action. It can delay an action without preventing it. It can fail behaviorally but remain as later evidence of foreknowledge. It can also work on the audience, accumulating the narrative conditions under which later punishment will look less arbitrary. A warning is therefore not a single signal with a single output. It changes epistemic and normative state even when it leaves physical behavior untouched.

Yet Amphinomus prevents us from equating knowledge with agency. The disguised Odysseus warns him that bloodshed is coming. Amphinomus leaves with foreboding, and the narrator then says that Athena has bound him to die at Telemachus's hands. The information may be correct while the practical exit is closed. Knowledge rises; control does not. Foreknowledge can therefore become tragic surplus rather than an affordance. This distinction will matter at Thrinakia, where the men know the prohibition but the material cost of obedience changes dramatically over time.

\section{Thrinakia: Consequence Under Constraint}

The Cattle of the Sun episode is the place where Nolan's scene and Homer's poem touch most directly. In Homer, the crew is explicitly warned. Odysseus extracts an oath. For as long as grain and wine remain, the cattle remain untouched. Then the weather continues to hold them on the island. Supplies disappear. The men fish, hunt birds, and take whatever they can. Eventually they are starving. Eurylochus proposes killing the cattle because death at sea is preferable to a slow death from famine \citep{HomerMurray1919}.

The sequence matters because the knowledge variable remains relatively stable while the constraint variable changes. The men do not suddenly forget the warning. Their feasible action set deteriorates. Early on, abstention is cheap because provisions exist. Later, abstention has become starvation. The same prohibition is therefore encountered under different material states. ``They chose'' is true but underspecified. A choice among acceptable alternatives is not the same structure as a choice among catastrophes.

This does not make the slaughter innocent. Homer frames it as \textit{atasthalia}, and Helios demands repayment. But the sequence stops us from treating foreknowledge as a complete theory of culpability. It raises a harder question: what counts as a viable alternative? Aristotle later calls some actions under severe pressure ``mixed,'' using the example of a sailor who voluntarily throws cargo overboard in a storm even though no one would choose that action under ordinary conditions. The comparison is historically later and should not be projected backward as Homer's doctrine. It nevertheless clarifies the variable Homer has already dramatized: voluntariness can be indexed to circumstance rather than attached to an action in the abstract.

Nolan makes the same pressure explicit in the Athena scene. The men say they decided they would rather drown than starve. Their statement does not deny the prophecy. It accepts a possible consequence because another consequence has become intolerable. This is precisely where the phrase ``chance or consequence'' becomes too blunt. The men can knowingly select an action that leads toward the predicted outcome without that making every cause of their deaths identical to their choice. Hunger, weather, divine punishment, Odysseus's decision to land, the men's decision to kill, and the cosmology of Helios and Zeus coexist in the same event.

The scene therefore shifts the problem from whether an action was chosen to how the choice architecture was produced. If leaders are responsible for anything beyond their own bodily motions, they may be responsible for information, routes, permissions, constraints, or institutional arrangements that shape other people's options. But even that broader responsibility is not sovereignty. The men remain agents inside a field they did not design completely.

\section{Odysseus Is Better at Reading Other People's Consequences}

The Cyclops episode contains the poem's asymmetry in miniature. After blinding Polyphemus, Odysseus can interpret the monster's suffering as deserved. Polyphemus violated hospitality, murdered guests, and now experiences repayment. Odysseus sees consequence clearly when he is explaining why another agent suffers.

Then the crew begs him to stop taunting the Cyclops. He ignores them. He reveals his name and lineage. The disclosure gives Polyphemus what ``Nobody'' had withheld: an address to which retaliation can be routed. The Cyclops can now invoke Poseidon against a specific person and a specific nostos. Odysseus's speech changes the causal reach of the injury.

The episode does not show a man who cannot understand causation. It shows a man whose causal intelligence is asymmetrical. He reads backward better than he reads forward. He can identify the antecedents of Polyphemus's suffering while exempting his own expressive act from the same model. The boast is not merely an error of pride. It converts an anonymous grievance into an addressable claim.

This asymmetry explains the force of Nolan's Athena. By the time he reaches the cattle episode, Odysseus has accumulated an archive of situations in which decisions escape the ends for which they were made. The problem is no longer that he thinks actions have no consequences. He has gone too far in the other direction. He has learned to see consequence everywhere and then to make himself its universal origin. Athena must teach him not less causality, but better causal granularity.

\section{From Reading Consequences to Designing Them}

The poem changes causal regime when Odysseus reaches Ithaca. In the Wanderings, the recurrent problem is how to read what has happened: Why did the wind fail? Why did the men die? What does a prophecy mean? Which action triggered divine hostility? From Book 13 onward, Athena and Odysseus increasingly reverse the direction of analysis. They begin with a desired terminal state and construct conditions that will make it possible.

Disguise controls identity information. Odysseus reveals himself selectively. Telemachus relocates weapons. Eumaeus and Philoetius are authenticated before joining the plan. Doors are controlled. The bow moves through a carefully staged sequence until it reaches the only person able to exploit it fully. The suitors occupy the same room while possessing a worse model of the room's actual state. Consequence becomes partly an information design problem.

This matters because the \textit{Odyssey} does not permit a simple moral distinction between passive victims of consequence and active causes. Odysseus is both. In Books 9 to 12 he is repeatedly caught inside causal fields he does not control. In Books 13 to 22 he becomes exceptionally skilled at building causal fields for other people. \textit{Metis} is not omnipotence; it is the art of changing local conditions so that some outcomes become easier and others harder.

The revenge scene then asks how much responsibility follows from participation in a collectively engineered wrong. After Antinous dies, Eurymachus does not claim innocence. He proposes a causal partition. Antinous, he says, was the principal organizer. He is already dead. The others can repay the material losses. Odysseus rejects the partition and the remedy. The dispute is therefore not only over whether harm occurred. It is over where the causal chain should stop, how responsibility should propagate across a group, and what quantity of repayment fits the wrong.

Loney's study of revenge in the poem makes this instability central. \textit{Tisis} is not merely an event that follows an offense. It is a narrative form of negative reciprocity. It requires an offense, an offender, a victim, an authorized avenger, a calculation of repayment, and some claim about when the account is settled \citep{Loney2019Ethics}. Because those roles can be redescribed, the same killing can be narrated as just repayment by one party and as a new offense by another.

The poem's consequences are therefore not just caused. They are assigned.

\section{Recognition: When the Past Becomes Evidence}

Recognition episodes reveal the epistemic side of the same architecture. Odysseus returns in a body that does not transparently announce its history. The problem becomes how present evidence can establish continuity with an absent past. Scars, stories, the bow, the bed, and remembered details work as traces. They do not merely symbolize identity; they provide access to production history.

The bed is especially revealing. Odysseus knows how it was made because he made it around the rooted olive tree. His response does more than name a secret. It demonstrates participation in the causal history of a durable object. Yet Bruno Currie's reading of Book 23 warns against collapsing all recognition into identity verification. Currie distinguishes knowing who someone is from acknowledging that knowledge and from restoring the emotional relation appropriate to it \citep{Currie2022Recognizing}. Causal proof can establish continuity without automatically restoring trust or intimacy.
